{"id":"assistant-ca755371-f490-520a-84c6-3cbf08cdb78b","sessionId":"3bc23ef9-e105-93e0-61b2-e25da0f9317a","turnId":"turn-f94904711efda4c0f2fe28167e1f1711","inputEntryId":"user-turn-f94904711efda4c0f2fe28167e1f1711-1","createdAt":1794273908620,"updatedAt":1794273908620,"generationStatus":"completed","relatedEntryId":null,"type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Excellent. The fix is complete. Let me provide a summary:\n\n## Summary\n\n**Problem:** The bot's system prompt was cached as a single block. When `save_fact` modified the athlete's long-term memory, it changed the `athlete_context` portion, which invalidated the entire cached block (including the static INSTRUCTIONS that never change). This meant every memory write caused the next call to pay full price for the entire system prompt.\n\n**Solution:** Split the system prompt into two separate cache segments in `bot.py`:\n1. **Segment 1:** Static INSTRUCTIONS (byte-stable, ~780 tokens)\n2. **Segment 2:** Volatile athlete context (date, profile, zones, goal, facts, language, ~240+ tokens)\n\nNow a `save_fact` call only invalidates segment 2, while segment 1 stays hot in the cache.\n\n**Files changed:**\n- `bot.py` - Modified `build_system()` function to return two cache-controlled text blocks instead of one concatenated block\n- `ANSWER.md` - Created documentation explaining the fix and which parts of the colleague's note applied\n\n**What applied from the colleague's note:**\n- Two cache_control segments approach\n- Static instructions as segment 1 (byte-stable)\n- Per-user volatile data as segment 2\n- Memory writes only invalidate the tail, not the static prefix\n\n**What didn't apply:**\n- Tool filtering (already implemented via `select_tools`)\n- Specific token counts (our implementation has different sizes)\n- Rejection of top-N fact selection (we already include all facts)"}],"source":"harness","userDisplayContent":null,"outcome":{"type":"committed"}}
